cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Alignment error type, not error rate, limits handwriting BCI decoding
message: >-
  If you use this work, please cite it. Please also cite the underlying dataset
  and article by Willett et al. (2021), listed under references: the
  zero-corruption reference condition in every experiment here is their
  forced-alignment output.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Aaron
    family-names: Ho
repository-code: >-
  https://github.com/AaronHo3/neural_decoding_imagined_handwriting
abstract: >-
  A controlled study of how decoder accuracy in brain-to-text handwriting BCI
  depends on the quality of its forced-alignment labels. Controlled corruption
  is injected into frame-level character labels across two corruption models,
  five severity levels, three decoder architectures, three seeds and two
  sequence-length regimes (198 runs). Boundary timing errors are shown to be
  nearly free up to 17 percent of frames, while character identity errors cost
  accuracy immediately, a 2.4x to 10.8x asymmetry. A secondary methodological
  result shows that sequence truncation compresses character error rate toward
  a ceiling and can invert an architecture conclusion.
keywords:
  - brain-computer interface
  - neural decoding
  - forced alignment
  - reproducibility
  - handwriting
license: MIT
